                [
                    604.0,
                    893.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "Early studies in collecting objective emotional labels were annotated by experts. Aman and Szpakowicz (2007) labeled each sentence of English blog posts with Ekman's six emotions and their intensity on a three-point scale. Strapparava and Mihalcea (2007) labeled Ekman's six emotional intensities to English news headlines and held a competition of SemEval-2007 Task 14. $ ^{5} $",
            "block_bbox": [
                605,
                894,
                1056,
                1110
            ],
            "block_id": 8,
            "block_order": 7,
            "group_id": 8,
            "global_block_id": 25,
            "global_group_id": 25,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    605.0,
                    894.0
                ],
                [
                    1056.0,
                    894.0
                ],
                [
                    1056.0,
                    1110.0
                ],
                [
                    605.0,
                    1110.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "In recent years, there have been many studies on collecting objective emotional labels using crowdsourcing. Mohammad and Bravo-Marquez (2017a); Mohammad and Kiritchenko (2018) labeled tweets in English, Arabic, and Spanish with the intensity of four emotions (joy, sadness, anger, and fear). Using these datasets, they held a series of competitions to estimate the emotional intensity in WASSA-2017 Shared Task on Emotion Intensity $ ^{6} $ (Mohammad and Bravo-Marquez, 2017b) and SemEval-2018 Task 1 $ ^{7} $ (Mohammad et al., 2018).",
